## How Idempotency Works
Order creation accepts an `Idempotency-Key` header. Before creating an
order, the service checks `idempotency_keys` for that customer + key
combination. If found, the original order response is returned instead
of creating a duplicate. The key is stored only after a successful
commit, scoped uniquely per customer (`UNIQUE(customerid, idempotencykey)`).

## How Stock Reservation Works
Order creation runs inside a single database transaction:
1. Every product row is locked with `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`.
2. Status and available stock are validated per item.
3. If everything is valid, `availableqty` decreases and `reservedqty`
   increases for each product, the order + order_items are inserted,
   and an inventory-history row is recorded — all in the same transaction.
4. If any item fails validation, the whole transaction rolls back — no
   partial orders, no partial stock changes.

## How Concurrency Is Handled
`SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` places a row-level lock on each product being
reserved. If two requests try to reserve the same product at the same
time, the second request's transaction waits until the first commits or
rolls back — so two customers can never reserve the same final unit.

## How Notification Retries Work
Notifications are queued (status `PENDING`) instantly when an order
event happens — the API does not wait for email delivery. A background
worker (every 30 seconds) picks up `PENDING`/`FAILED` notifications with
`retrycount < 3`, attempts delivery, and marks them `SENT` or increments
`retrycount` on failure. After 3 failed attempts, a notification stops
being retried.

